Question: What does static mean in C#?

Answer: 
In C#, the static keyword is used to declare members of a class that are associated with the class itself rather than with instances of the class. When a member (such as a method, field, or property) is marked as static, it belongs to the class rather than to any specific object created from the class. This means that you can access the static member without creating an instance of the class.

For example, consider a class with a static method:
public class MyClass {
    public static void MyStaticMethod() {
        // code here
    }
}
You can call MyStaticMethod without creating an instance of MyClass:
MyClass.MyStaticMethod();
Static members are also shared among all instances of the class. If a static field is modified, the change is visible to all instances of the class.

Note that static methods cannot access instance members because they do not have access to an instance of the class. They can only access static members.

Static members are often used for utility functions, constants, or methods that need to be accessed without creating an object.
